      "id": "ES-C342-minuto",
      "sequence": 6020,
      "title": "el minuto — the minute, the first small cutting of the hour",
      "headword": "el minuto",
      "romanization": "el minuto",
      "gloss": "the minute — Latin minutus, made small, from minuere to lessen; English minute, minor, minus, diminish and minuscule are the same smallness",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:df9c0fcda2ab381d",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say behind, then inside. (Detrás; dentro.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la hora — the thing this one cuts up."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el reloj — what shows it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mi-NU-to, the weight on the middle beat. Every vowel is short and clean; the u is always oo, never the English yoo. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el minuto — \"the minute.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin minūtus meant made small — the past participle of minuere, to lessen. It says nothing about clocks at all."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The clock meaning came from a piece of medieval bookkeeping. When astronomers divided the hour, they called the first cut pars minūta prīma, the first small part, and the cut after that pars minūta secunda, the second small part. Both names got shortened to their odd halves: the first small part became the minute, and the second small part became the second."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which means the word you already have for second and this new one are two halves of the same phrase, and neither one was ever a time word."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English keeps the smallness plainly. Something minor is smaller. Minus takes away. To diminish is to make less. Minuscule is tiny. And minute spelled exactly the same way, said my-NEWT, still means very small — the original sense, sitting in plain sight."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The hour, cut into its first small parts."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el minuto",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el minuto*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "un minuto, por favor",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *un minuto, por favor*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cinco minutos",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cinco minutos*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did minūtus mean? (Made small.) And what was the full name of the minute? (Pars minūta prīma — the first small part.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C342-cuarto",
      "sequence": 6030,
      "title": "el cuarto — the quarter, and the four you already count",
      "headword": "el cuarto",
      "romanization": "el cuarto",
      "gloss": "the quarter, a fourth — Latin quartus, fourth, on quattuor four; English quarter, quart, quartet and quarantine all count to four",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:1ef8e75bd047ebd1",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say inside, then the minute. (Dentro; el minuto.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco — the number this is built on."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el minuto — the other piece of the hour."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "KWAR-to, two beats with the weight on the first. The cu- is kw, and -uar- glides in one motion. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el cuarto — \"the quarter,\" \"a fourth.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have counted cuatro for a long time. This is that number turned into an order: Latin quattuor, four, gives quārtus, fourth, and Spanish wears that down to cuarto."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So cuatro and cuarto are one letter apart on the page and one step apart in meaning: the count, and the position in the count. Hearing the difference is worth practising, because the two are easy to swap."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English counts the same way. A quarter is a fourth. A quart is a fourth of a gallon. A quartet is four players. And quarantine is quaranta giorni, forty days — forty being four tens, the period ships once waited out before landing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "On a clock, y cuarto is quarter past: la una y cuarto. Spanish also uses cuarto for a room, which is the same idea — a quarter of a house divided into four."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A quarter of the hour, from the four you already had."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el cuarto",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el cuarto*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "un cuarto",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *un cuarto*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la una y cuarto",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la una y cuarto*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which number is cuarto built on? (Cuatro, four.) And what is quarantine, word for word? (Forty days.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C342-punto",
      "sequence": 6040,
      "title": "el punto — the point, from the prick of a needle",
      "headword": "el punto",
      "romanization": "el punto",
      "gloss": "the point, the dot — Latin punctum, a prick, from pungere to prick; English point, puncture, punctual, punctuation and pungent share the needle",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:477f7f5557416f63",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the minute, then the quarter. (El minuto; el cuarto.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la hora — what this pins down exactly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el cuarto — the last cut of the hour."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "PUN-to, the weight on the first beat. The n before t stays a clean n, and the u is oo. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el punto — \"the point,\" \"the dot.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin pungere meant to prick, to pierce. What a prick leaves behind is a punctum — a tiny mark made by something sharp. That is the whole word: a dot is the hole a needle made."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English carries the needle everywhere. A point is the sharp end. A puncture is the hole. Punctuation is the set of dots pricked into a sentence. Something pungent pricks your nose. And to be punctual is to arrive at the exact pricked spot on the dial — not near it, on it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish says the same thing with en punto: la una en punto is one o'clock on the dot, the hand standing exactly on the mark. English chose the identical picture without borrowing the phrase."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A punto is also the full stop at the end of a written sentence, for the same reason."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now an hour can be given exactly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el punto",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el punto*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "en punto",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *en punto*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la una en punto",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la una en punto*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did pungere mean? (To prick.) And what does it mean to be punctual? (To land on the exact pricked mark.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C342-horario",
      "sequence": 6050,
      "title": "el horario — the timetable, an hour-thing",
      "headword": "el horario",
      "romanization": "el horario",
      "gloss": "the timetable, the opening hours — Latin horarium, on hora an hour; English hour, horoscope and horology come off the same root, and the -ario ending is English -ary",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:2837b0be4e54329e",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the quarter, then the point, the dot. (El cuarto; el punto.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la hora — the word this is built on."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el reloj — what keeps the hours it lists."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "o-RA-rio, the weight on the second beat. The h is silent, always, and -rio is one syllable with a quick glide. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el horario — \"the timetable,\" \"the opening hours.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is hora, which you have had for a long time, plus the ending -ario — Latin -ārium, which makes a thing to do with. So a horarium is an hour-thing: the sheet that tells you which hours something happens in."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ending is worth collecting, because it is English -ary and -arium almost every time. A library is a book-thing, from liber. An aquarium is a water-thing. A granary is a grain-thing. And a horarium is an hour-thing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The hora half is English hour — the same word, with the h gone quiet in both languages and the vowel wandering. It is also the horo- of horoscope, which is a reading of the hour you were born in, and of horology, the study of clocks."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A horario is what you ask for at a station or a shop: ¿cuál es el horario?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From the hour itself to the sheet that lists them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
