          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the point, the dot, then the timetable. (El punto; el horario.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mediodía, medianoche — where this stretch begins."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la noche — the stretch it closes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ma-dru-GA-da, four beats with the weight on the third. The d between vowels is very soft, close to the th of this. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la madrugada — the small hours, the stretch between midnight and daybreak."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish has a whole word for a time English can only describe. English says the small hours or the early morning and needs several words. Spanish says la madrugada and is done."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It comes from the verb madrugar, to get up very early, and behind that stands Latin mātūrus — ripe. A ripe fruit is one that came to its proper time, and Latin used maturus for anything early, timely, ahead of schedule. Somebody who rose in the dark was rising while the day was still ripening."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English kept the fruit. Something mature is ripe, whether it is cheese or judgement. Something premature arrived before it had ripened. And to maturate is to ripen."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So this stretch of the night is not named for its darkness. It is named for being early, and early was named for being ripe."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five pieces of the clock: the minute, the quarter, the point, the timetable, and the hours before dawn."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
